A||||6||Mikey||Weird Electrical Problem
Z||000000||Mikey||02-22-99||04:54 PM||||   Now this is weird.  My radio will not keep it's memory! If you set the stations, or the time, then turn the car off, when you turn it back on, everything is reset.  I know the battery is fine as it will start the car, and I had just recharged it.<br>  <br>   Now the wierd part. If you have the drivers door closed when you set the radio, turn it off and on again, the memory is NOT reset! However if you have the drivers door open (or the overhead map light turned on) when you turn the car off and on again, the memeory is lost!!<p>   Any ideas?<p>Mike<br>
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||02-24-99||01:16 AM||||This is usually as simple as a blown fuse. Some systems are internally tied together which means that if one circuit blows, it is partially powered by another until that circuit is actually used.<p>So right now your radio is getting power from the same circuit as the doors (because of a blown fuse) but as soon as you open the doors, the doors draw too much current from the circuit to power your radio as well. Check your fuses, I'll bet you have a blown one!
Z||000002||ccmarsig||02-25-99||11:44 AM||||I had this happen for a while as well.  I traced my problem down to a moisture problem because water was leaking in from the grill over my battery...the thing'd just short out every so often because of this, especially after rain...I put a plastic bag over my battery and eveything now works fine.  Now if I can figure out why my fan stays on after shutting the thing down lately I'll be happy.<p>clifyt
Z||000003||Cliff Pennock||02-25-99||11:55 AM||||Your fan stays on after you turned off ignition? Now that *is* weird. I'll see what I can find.
Z||000004||ccmarsig||02-25-99||01:29 PM||||I had to get my car jumped the other day because of this problem.  I thought I had left the compartment lights on...after getting the battery juiced up I stopped at home and heard the fan running.  I had to open the hood to check this out.  Started the engine and shut down a few times til the thing would quit spinning.  Hell, I even stuck my hand in to stop the spinning to see if it was just the inertia.  I'd let go and it start again.  This is a relatively recent problem and though annoying I'm not too upset.  I've always had a few screwy electrical probs in this thing.<p>clifyt
Z||000005||iskydiveto||03-01-99||10:03 AM||||The fan did that in My '84 too. Turns out that the fan relay (left hand side off radiator, under headlight housing) is the culprit. Over time the seals on it wear out and allow moisture inside. The contacts corrode and BAM, fan run all by itself, and usually slower than normal.Replace the relay, it's the big one, and the problem should be solved.<br>Jim<br>
Z||000006||Carrolles||04-22-99||09:05 AM||||I comute between Birmingham and St. Louis every week and leave my Fiero at the airport.  On one trip my alternator went out on the way to the airport.  Hte battery died as I pulled into the airport parking lot.  While in St. Louis I pondered how to get my car home on Friday.  I knew that simply getting a jump start would not do since the fuel pump uses about 10 or 15 amps to run.<br>I bought a jumper battery at Sam's for $39.99.  It is a gel/acid battery about the size of a boom box radio and has two battery jumper cables about 18" long.<br>Being a gel/acid battery you don't have to worry about it spilling acid if it turns over.  The electrolyte is actually a paste material.<br>I put the jumper battery in the trunk and hooked my regular jumper cables between it and the car battery and drove home with no problems.  The jumper battery has about 800 cold cranking amps and weighs about 10 pounds.  It also has a cigarette lighter type plug so you can plug in accessories.<br>I keep this battery in my trunk at all times and have had to use it a couple of times at the airport.<br>I know this has nothing to do with your problem but it is good insurance to have in an old car especially if you have to leave it parked for a while.<br>Good luck,<br>Carroll
